Hi Hadley,

On 2009-May-08  , at 14:06 , hadley wickham wrote:

>> So the bug would be that range.circular does not accept the second
>> NULL
>> argument, is that it?
>> In that case, I am not sure what this NULL argument is in the case
>> of the
>> regular range, which syntax is supposed to be:
>>      range(..., na.rm = FALSE)
>>      ## Default S3 method:
>>      range(..., na.rm = FALSE, finite = FALSE)
>> what is the reason to use NULL (and not T/F)?
>

> It's the ...
>
> You can call range like range(1, 2, 3) and you'd expect to get the
> same result as range(1, 2, 3, NULL)

OK, I fixed range.circular (and other functions it called) however I
am still hitting plenty of issues. I think the design of the circular
class is kind of flawed and there are important issues such as:

x <- circular(1, units="radians")
y <- circular(1, units="degrees")
x+y
 > x+y
Circular Data:
Type = angles
Units = radians
Template = none
Modulo = asis
Zero = 0
Rotation = counter
[1] 2

It does not do the conversion between the two ways of measuring angles.

If addition (and subtraction, and ...) do not work I guess that plenty
of other functions end up broken. So for now I'll just convert them to
numeric.

Thank anyway for your help.

JiHO
